From edd64c80c815639959053e1c0f07fe977c121691 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gavin John <gavinnjohn@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 20:04:12 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 39/44] aout: bound the drift correction in cents, not permille

The bound on the drift correction was a permille of the sample rate, which
says nothing about the thing it exists to limit. Resampling corrects drift
by changing the rate, and changing the rate changes pitch; what has to stay
inaudible is how far out of tune it pushes the material. State it as one:
cents, hundredths of a semitone. The default of 9 cents is 5.2 permille,
against the 5 permille it replaces, so what it allows is all but unchanged.

The figure is kept as an interval rather than converted to a rate offset
when the filters are built, because the clamp has to be applied in that
unit. Cents are logarithmic: a ratio of 1 + x is a smaller interval than
1 - x, so bounding the offset symmetrically allows more detuning downwards
than up, and by a different amount at every rate. Bounding the interval is
symmetric by construction.

The controller works in cents throughout for the same reason - its own
clamp on the integral has to mean the same thing as the clamp on its
output, or the two disagree about where the bound is.

What reaches the resampler is not what the controller asks for: it is a
whole number of Hz, and it may have been clamped. Integrating against the
request would make the integral answer for a correction that was never
applied - winding up while pinned at the bound, and accumulating the
rounding either side of it. aout_FiltersSetDetune() returns the detune it
managed, and the difference is fed back over the time constant the gains
already imply, so the integral tracks what the output is actually doing.

"aout-max-resampling" keeps its name and its meaning of zero (no
correction), and no previously valid setting now asks for more detuning
than it did. It was added earlier on this branch and has never been in a
release, so nothing in the field is set in permille.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
